  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Reciprocal and Ratio Relations",
    "description": "Work with x + 1/x style givens and chained ratios to find related values without solving for the variable.",
    "teachingApproach": "Open by splitting a mixed number into x + 1/x, then climb the ladder: square and subtract 2, subtract 2 again for the minus form, cube for the third powers. Then switch to chained ratios, ratio-to-expression evaluation by dividing through, and the a + b = 2c collapse. Fresh question and parallel retry after every recipe.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 26,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 4, Simplification. Approved scope: Teach reciprocal relations: reading x + 1/x = 17/4 as 4 1/4 to guess x = 4, squaring x + 1/x to get x^2 + 1/x^2 (subtract 2), and then subtracting 2 again to reach (x − 1/x)^2 and x − 1/x, exactly as the book's x + 1/x = 17/4 → x − 1/x = 15/4 example. Extend to x^3 + 1/x^3 = (x + 1/x)^3 − 3(x + 1/x). Cover chained ratios a/b and b/c multiplying to a/c, and using a known ratio such as c/a = 4/3 to evaluate (c^2 − a^2)/(c^2 + a^2) by dividing through. Include symmetric conditions such as a + b = 2c where a − c = −(b − c) collapses an expression like a/(a − c) + c/(b − c) to 1. Learners should avoid solving for the variable when the expression can be evaluated directly from the given relation."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn the questions where you never actually find x.\n\nThe exam gives you x + 1/x or a/b and asks for something related. The art is reshaping what you know — not solving for what you don't.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's meet a strange-looking creature:\n\nx + 1/x = 17/4\n\n1/x just means the flip of x. The equation says: a number plus its own flip makes 17/4.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Turn 17/4 into a mixed number:\n\n17/4 = 4 1/4 = 4 + 1/4\n\nSee it? A number (4) plus its flip (1/4).\n\nSo x = 4. The mixed number splits directly into the answer.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
